 "title": "Letter Combinations of a Phone Number",
 "category": "Backtracking",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "description": "You are given a string `digits` made of the digits `2` to `9`. On a phone keypad each digit maps to letters: 2 = abc, 3 = def, 4 = ghi, 5 = jkl, 6 = mno, 7 = pqrs, 8 = tuv, 9 = wxyz.\n\nReturn every letter string the digits could spell, in any order. If `digits` is empty, return an empty list.\n\n**Constraints**\n\n- `0 <= len(digits) <= 4`",
 "function": "letterCombinations",
 "params": [
  "digits"
 ],
 "starter": "class Solution:\n    def letterCombinations(self, digits: str) -> List[str]:\n        pass\n",
 "compare": "unordered",
 "hints": [
  "Each digit adds one letter choice. How many strings are there in total?",
  "Build the strings one digit at a time, either recursively or by extending a list of partial strings.",
  "Start with `[\"\"]` and, for each digit, replace the list with every partial string plus every letter for that digit. Remember the empty-input case."
 ],
